WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:33.240
Announcer: From the nation's capital, the American
Enterprise Institute For Public Policy Research

00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:39.890
presents "Public Policy Forums", a series
of programs featuring the nation's top authorities,

00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:43.979
presenting their differing views on the vital
issues which confront us.

00:00:43.979 --> 00:00:49.159
The topic, "Freedom of the Press: The First
Amendment Protections."

00:00:49.159 --> 00:00:54.079
Should reporters be forced by the courts to
reveal confidential sources of information?

00:00:54.080 --> 00:01:00.200
To what extent should the First Amendment
protect the press from libel suits by private citizens?

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:02.819
Now, here is Peter Hackes.

00:01:02.819 --> 00:01:08.370
Peter Hackes: In this bicentennial era as we
reexamine some of the great principles on

00:01:08.370 --> 00:01:14.409
which this country is based, freedom of the
press stands out as a cornerstone of our democracy.

00:01:14.409 --> 00:01:18.590
From time to time, that word freedom has to
be spelled out in detail if the First Amendment

00:01:18.590 --> 00:01:22.030
to the Constitution is to have some specific
meaning.

00:01:22.030 --> 00:01:27.450
Is the press free, for example, to print any
item, even if it might damage the individual

00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:29.859
rights of a certain person?

00:01:29.859 --> 00:01:34.500
Is the press free not to print certain items
because a government agency says it might

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:37.149
be a threat to national security?

00:01:37.149 --> 00:01:40.649
And who decides these matters, the newspaper,
the government, the court?

00:01:40.649 --> 00:01:45.689
Should a reporter be privileged, protected
by a legal shield, against being forced to

00:01:45.689 --> 00:01:47.640
reveal his news sources?

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:50.920
Does the public have an absolute right to
know?

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:55.729
From the National Press Club in Washington,
welcome to another roundtable discussion presented

00:01:55.729 --> 00:02:01.840
by the American Enterprise Institute, a nonprofit,
nonpartisan, research and education organization.

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:06.890
Today's guests are well-versed in the topic,
Freedom of the Press: The First Amendment

00:02:06.890 --> 00:02:08.100
Protections."

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:13.530
Members of our panels are Charles Seib, Associate
Editor of the "Washington Post".

00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:17.250
Mr. Seib has been a reporter or editor for
the "Associated Press", "Gannett News Service",

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:19.360
and "The Washington Star".

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:25.170
Floyd Abrams, a partner in a New York law
firm which specializes in communications law.

00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:28.540
He has filed many briefs in First Amendment
court cases.

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:34.750
Antonin Scalia, an Assistant U.S. Attorney
General involved in press freedom cases.

00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:39.830
Mr. Scalia served as general counsel in the
White House Office of Telecommunications and

00:02:39.830 --> 00:02:42.330
is chairman of the Administrative Conference.

00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:46.970
Edward Epstein, director of a research project
on news agencies sponsored by the Twentieth

00:02:46.970 --> 00:02:48.340
Century Fund.

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:51.290
He's the author of the book, "News from Nowhere".

00:02:51.290 --> 00:02:55.320
Jack Nelson is Washington Bureau Chief for
the "Los Angeles Times".

00:02:55.320 --> 00:03:00.180
He's a Pulitzer Prize winner for his investigative
reporting while serving as a reporter for

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:01.770
the "Atlanta Constitution".

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:05.030
Moderating our discussion is William Ruckelshaus.

00:03:05.030 --> 00:03:09.560
Mr. Ruckelshaus served as Deputy U.S. Attorney
General and is head of the Civil Division

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:10.810
of the Justice Department.

00:03:10.810 --> 00:03:15.920
He also headed the FBI and the Environmental
Protection Agency.

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:18.580
Mr. Ruckelshaus practices law in Washington,
DC.

00:03:18.580 --> 00:03:20.590
Now, our moderator, Mr. Ruckelshaus.

00:03:20.590 --> 00:03:26.010
William Ruckelshaus: Thank you, Mr. Hackes.

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:31.890
The First Amendment to the United States Constitution
states, "Congress shall make no law abridging

00:03:31.890 --> 00:03:33.000
freedom of the press."

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:39.980
Throughout our history, the meaning of those
words and the nature of the freedom it protects

00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:41.610
have caused controversy.

00:03:41.610 --> 00:03:47.910
Tonight, we're going to discuss with our distinguished
panel, and later the audience, three current

00:03:47.910 --> 00:03:54.240
controversies whose common theme involves
the tension between the First Amendment and

00:03:54.240 --> 00:04:00.230
the government's exercise of power through
the judicial system.

00:04:00.230 --> 00:04:01.230
Newsman's privilege.

00:04:01.230 --> 00:04:08.080
Should a reporter, under any circumstances,
be forced by the courts to reveal their confidential

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:10.200
sources of information?

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:16.150
The Supreme Court in 1972, in the case of
Branzburg against Hayes, said under certain

00:04:16.150 --> 00:04:22.700
circumstances a newsman could be forced to
testify before a grand jury and forced to

00:04:22.700 --> 00:04:26.610
disclose the identity of their confidential
news sources.

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:32.940
Since that time, and really before, several
so-called shield laws have been introduced

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:40.340
in Congress and in the states to protect the
confidentiality of a newsman's source from

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:42.160
judicial interference.

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:43.280
Libel.

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:48.300
To what extent does, or should, the First
Amendment to the United States Constitution

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:54.470
protect the press from libel actions by private
citizens claiming they were wronged?

00:04:54.470 --> 00:05:00.290
Is there, or should there be, a difference
between the protections provided a private

00:05:00.290 --> 00:05:02.870
citizen and a public figure?

00:05:02.870 --> 00:05:10.510
Lastly, to what extent should the courts prohibit
the publication of classified materials?

00:05:10.510 --> 00:05:15.570
Starting with the Pentagon Papers case and
continuing with the current sensational revelations

00:05:15.570 --> 00:05:20.500
about our intelligence-gathering agencies,
are there any limits in the name of national

00:05:20.500 --> 00:05:24.810
security to what may be published?

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:29.960
In no particular order, let's start with newsman's
privilege.

00:05:29.960 --> 00:05:30.960
Mr. Nelson, let me ask you.

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:36.890
Should the Congress of the United States pass
a shield law absolutely protecting the rights

00:05:36.890 --> 00:05:41.920
of a newsman not to reveal his confidential
news sources anywhere in the country?

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:46.480
Jack Nelson: Well, I think the Congress should
pass such a law and I think it should be an

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:48.240
absolute law, an unqualified law.

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:54.700
I would be opposed to any sort of law that
had qualifications and that might open such

00:05:54.700 --> 00:05:57.270
a bill to amendment in the future.

00:05:57.270 --> 00:06:05.120
And I'd like to just say, by way of background,
I became a member of a group called the Reporters'

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:08.760
Committee for Freedom of the Press, which
was formed after Earl Caldwell was subpoenaed

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:13.620
in the first case where the Justice Department
began to subpoena reporters during the Nixon

00:06:13.620 --> 00:06:14.620
administration.

00:06:14.620 --> 00:06:18.650
This committee, formed of working reporters,
mostly in Washington, saw that there was a

00:06:18.650 --> 00:06:25.120
necessity to protect the reporters and their
confidential sources in cases, particularly

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:27.900
where investigation of government is concerned.

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:34.300
And the reporters' committee has been in favor
from the outset of an unqualified law.

00:06:34.300 --> 00:06:40.500
Now, I think, for example, that if the Nixon
administration, for example, had been able

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:46.840
to subpoena Woodward and Bernstein of the
Post, or other reporters, and learned confidential

00:06:46.840 --> 00:06:52.340
sources, you would have never had Watergate
uncovered.

00:06:52.340 --> 00:06:56.441
And there are many other stories that you
can look at that would have never have come

00:06:56.441 --> 00:07:01.890
out had reporters been subpoenaed and had
they'd been forced to divulge confidential

00:07:01.890 --> 00:07:02.890
sources.

00:07:02.890 --> 00:07:05.220
William Ruckelshaus: Mr. Scalia, you're from the
Department of Justice.

00:07:05.220 --> 00:07:09.440
Do you have a contrary view to that, or do
you support Mr. Nelson?

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:17.550
Antonin Scalia: Well, I think Mr. Nelson's view
is the most rational, next to what I think

00:07:17.550 --> 00:07:23.169
is the correct one, and that is that there
should not be a newsman's privilege law.

00:07:23.169 --> 00:07:27.120
The reason I say it's the most logical next
to that is that there are many who think well,

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:30.010
we should have a newsman's privilege law but
it should be qualified.

00:07:30.010 --> 00:07:32.821
And that, I think, is the worst of both worlds.

00:07:32.821 --> 00:07:36.660
The problem is, if you have one that's unqualified,
you just can't live with it.

00:07:36.660 --> 00:07:39.110
And if you have one that's qualified, it just
doesn't work.

00:07:39.110 --> 00:07:46.470
So, I think the best state of laws is the
one that we now have in which you cannot get

00:07:46.470 --> 00:07:49.130
a newsman to testify in court all the time.

00:07:49.130 --> 00:07:52.160
There are judicial protections against harassment.

00:07:52.160 --> 00:07:56.230
But on the other hand, there is no instance
in which a newsman can go in and say categorically,

00:07:56.230 --> 00:08:00.770
"The law gives me an absolute privilege not
to appear."

00:08:00.770 --> 00:08:05.229
It really doesn't help the discussion to put
the whole thing in the context of the Nixon

00:08:05.229 --> 00:08:07.419
administration subpoenaing newsmen.

00:08:07.419 --> 00:08:12.370
It normally isn't the Nixon administration,
it's normally a court in a criminal case.

00:08:12.370 --> 00:08:16.021
And as often as not, they're subpoenaing the
newsmen not on behalf of the government, but

00:08:16.021 --> 00:08:17.160
on behalf of the defendant.

00:08:17.160 --> 00:08:21.260
Jack Nelson: I would agree with you that we
shouldn't necessarily put it just in the context

00:08:21.260 --> 00:08:24.750
of the Nixon administration, but you've got
to realize that the subpoenaing of reporters

00:08:24.750 --> 00:08:29.680
did not begin though by the Justice Department
until the Nixon administration.

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:32.070
Antonin Scalia: I doubt very much whether that's
true.

00:08:32.070 --> 00:08:37.199
I don't know the facts, all the facts on how
many newsmen's subpoenas there have ever been

00:08:37.199 --> 00:08:38.199
in the history of American jurisprudence.

00:08:38.199 --> 00:08:42.769
But I find it difficult to imagine that a
newsman had never been subpoenaed on behalf

00:08:42.769 --> 00:08:46.920
of either the government or the defense in
any criminal trial prior to Nixon.

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:48.189
At least I just...

00:08:48.189 --> 00:08:51.579
Floyd Abrams: I can't give you a number, but
we did attempt to look into it and there's

00:08:51.579 --> 00:08:57.209
certainly our impression on reading every
case they could find, that prior to the Nixon

00:08:57.209 --> 00:09:00.709
administration there had been very, very few
cases and very, very few subpoenas issuing,

00:09:00.709 --> 00:09:03.389
and certainly out of the Department of Justice.

00:09:03.389 --> 00:09:09.319
That there was a new and to the press and
I think the public, a disastrous flow of subpoenas

00:09:09.319 --> 00:09:14.170
pouring out of the Department of Justice in
the early days of the Nixon administration.

00:09:14.170 --> 00:09:18.880
That has stopped certainly to a major extent.

00:09:18.880 --> 00:09:25.540
We do have, I'd like to add, a qualified privilege
now in law, as a matter of law, because of

00:09:25.540 --> 00:09:27.310
the cases that that has developed.

00:09:27.310 --> 00:09:33.800
I would agree with Mr. Nelson that an absolute
privilege would be a better idea.

00:09:33.800 --> 00:09:37.410
But I don't think really we should let pass
the idea that a qualified privilege doesn't

00:09:37.410 --> 00:09:38.410
do any good.

00:09:38.410 --> 00:09:39.509
We do have one now.

00:09:39.509 --> 00:09:45.879
Case after case is coming down, in court after
court around the country, which is establishing

00:09:45.879 --> 00:09:50.709
that in situations in which there are alternative
sources for example to get information, you

00:09:50.709 --> 00:09:51.800
can't go to the newsmen first.

00:09:51.800 --> 00:09:54.829
And if you really need it very much you can't
go to the newsmen first.

00:09:54.829 --> 00:09:55.829
Antonin Scalia: I don't have a problem with that.

00:09:55.829 --> 00:09:59.220
The Justice Department guidelines for subpoenaing
newsmen provide exactly that and I have no

00:09:59.220 --> 00:10:04.600
quarrel with that if you're talking about
a qualified privilege that is judicially created

00:10:04.600 --> 00:10:07.649
and administered or one that is administratively
created or administered.

00:10:07.649 --> 00:10:12.230
But I thought we were talking about legislation
that would establish a qualified privilege

00:10:12.230 --> 00:10:16.170
in the sense that you don't adjudge it on
a case-by-case basis, but you have some flat

00:10:16.170 --> 00:10:19.480
rule that will be applied in a certain category
of cases.

00:10:19.480 --> 00:10:24.139
I don't think that achieves what the main
purpose of the newsman's privilege law is

00:10:24.139 --> 00:10:30.010
intended to achieve and that is to open up
confidential sources and prevent the so-called

00:10:30.010 --> 00:10:32.819
chilling effect upon confidential sources
because...

00:10:32.819 --> 00:10:34.689
William Ruckelshaus: Mr. Seib has a remark.

00:10:34.689 --> 00:10:37.790
Charles Seib: I just wondered if this was a realistic
discussion.

00:10:37.790 --> 00:10:41.819
Is there any possibility at all of getting
an absolute privilege law out of Congress?

00:10:41.819 --> 00:10:44.540
Jack Nelson: No, I doubt seriously that there
is any possibility.

00:10:44.540 --> 00:10:46.999
Now, at one time, there seemed to be a possibility.

00:10:46.999 --> 00:10:52.661
Charles Seib: Well, once you concede that you
can't, wouldn't you rather stay with the First

00:10:52.661 --> 00:10:54.380
Amendment than get into a limited...

00:10:54.380 --> 00:10:56.970
Jack Nelson: I would and I think you said you
would, too but...

00:10:56.970 --> 00:10:58.290
Antonin Scalia: I said I would too.

00:10:58.290 --> 00:11:05.899
I have no quarrel with the fact that for two
reasons, one is that freedom of the press

00:11:05.899 --> 00:11:10.019
is a particularly important freedom under
our Constitution and it deserves some...it

00:11:10.019 --> 00:11:11.999
has some special constitutional protection.

00:11:11.999 --> 00:11:17.690
And the second one is that newsmen are particularly
subject to harassment by subpoenas because

00:11:17.690 --> 00:11:21.889
they're in the business of finding out, investigating
facts.

00:11:21.889 --> 00:11:24.149
Police departments could come to rely upon
them to do their jobs.

00:11:24.149 --> 00:11:28.040
For both of those reasons, I think it makes
sense to accord them some special protection

00:11:28.040 --> 00:11:33.319
through the courts and through the administrators,
such as the Justice Department not subpoenaing

00:11:33.319 --> 00:11:35.949
these men willy-nilly, which we don't do.

00:11:35.949 --> 00:11:40.000
But beyond that, I don't think any more is
needed or is, indeed, desirable.

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:43.490
William Ruckelshaus: Mr. Epstein, what's your
view on the privilege?

00:11:43.490 --> 00:11:51.130
Edward Epstein: I don't really think that to
clarify the issue and to try and pretend that

00:11:51.130 --> 00:11:56.470
there has been freedom of the press up to
the Nixon administration, and somehow the

00:11:56.470 --> 00:12:01.519
Nixon administration violated this freedom
of the press by subpoenaing reporters, is

00:12:01.519 --> 00:12:02.810
historically accurate.

00:12:02.810 --> 00:12:08.540
I think you have complete censorship in World
War II and the Korean War when newspapers

00:12:08.540 --> 00:12:12.949
and magazines were told what to publish on
the covers, what stories to publish, and they

00:12:12.949 --> 00:12:18.029
were threatened, editors were threatened by
being drafted in the army unless they went

00:12:18.029 --> 00:12:19.029
along.

00:12:19.029 --> 00:12:25.089
You had the McCarthy period stretching into
the early 50s when...and newsmen suddenly

00:12:25.089 --> 00:12:28.519
didn't have any sort of freedom to report
what they wanted to.

00:12:28.519 --> 00:12:33.189
And while you might say that you could find
very few cases of the government subpoenaing

00:12:33.189 --> 00:12:36.790
witnesses before the Nixon administration,
perhaps they didn't have to.

00:12:36.790 --> 00:12:43.069
But certainly, in private libel cases, hundreds
of reporters were subpoenaed by one side or

00:12:43.069 --> 00:12:46.059
the other and made to divulge their sources.

00:12:46.059 --> 00:12:51.730
And you said at the beginning of the discussion
that newsmen have a right and it's a question

00:12:51.730 --> 00:12:55.540
whether Congress should certify that right
by law.

00:12:55.540 --> 00:13:00.679
But the Supreme Court has never found that
newsman has a right not to testify if they're

00:13:00.679 --> 00:13:02.879
a witness to a crime.

00:13:02.879 --> 00:13:09.860
And the question really is, is it worth elevating
newsmen to some special status by giving them

00:13:09.860 --> 00:13:13.970
absolute privilege, which neither doctors
nor lawyers have?

00:13:13.970 --> 00:13:21.300
Mr. Nelson says Watergate would never have
been revealed if newsmen could have been subpoenaed.

00:13:21.300 --> 00:13:27.709
Well, I remember that Mr. Nelson, I think
had, I would say the single most important

00:13:27.709 --> 00:13:32.860
revelation on Watergate, as far as the public
was concerned, when he published an interview

00:13:32.860 --> 00:13:40.209
with an ex-FBI agent, Alfred Baldwin, which
detailed the entire Watergate crime, as seen

00:13:40.209 --> 00:13:45.720
from the man who was the intermediary between
the Watergate burglars and the conspirators,

00:13:45.720 --> 00:13:48.119
Hunt and Liddy.

00:13:48.119 --> 00:13:53.509
And you published this entire interview, and
if you had been subpoenaed--by the way, your

00:13:53.509 --> 00:13:57.769
editors were--but let's just say you had and
you went to court and you revealed that Baldwin

00:13:57.769 --> 00:14:00.069
was your source, I can't see that it would
have made any difference.

00:14:00.069 --> 00:14:01.600
Jack Nelson: Oh, I think it's a little different
story, though.

00:14:01.600 --> 00:14:02.600
Everybody knew Baldwin was the source.

00:14:02.600 --> 00:14:09.639
The point here was whether or not the tapes
of the interview should be turned over because

00:14:09.639 --> 00:14:12.790
the interview had been conducted in confidence.

00:14:12.790 --> 00:14:14.220
And I think a good point here is though...

00:14:14.220 --> 00:14:15.220
Edward Epstein: But they were turned over.

00:14:15.220 --> 00:14:17.809
Jack Nelson: Well, I know, but the point here
is that Baldwin, yeah, but Baldwin

00:14:17.809 --> 00:14:18.809
wanted them turned over.

00:14:18.809 --> 00:14:21.259
But let me tell you, though, I think that's
a very interesting point.

00:14:21.259 --> 00:14:25.709
We operated in an atmosphere there of an intimidation
of the government of the news media.

00:14:25.709 --> 00:14:30.869
For example, Ron Ostrow of the "LA Times"
was with me on that case, and the two of us

00:14:30.869 --> 00:14:35.899
knew, you know, in our own minds that the
government would try to get a hold of these

00:14:35.899 --> 00:14:36.899
tapes of the Baldwin interview.

00:14:36.899 --> 00:14:37.899
We felt sure they would.

00:14:37.899 --> 00:14:42.179
So, when we came back to Washington, we sent
the tapes to Los Angeles, just in order to

00:14:42.179 --> 00:14:44.220
keep the government from getting them.

00:14:44.220 --> 00:14:50.421
Now, what happened immediately thereafter
was that Earl J. Silbert, who was the original

00:14:50.421 --> 00:14:51.629
prosecutor of the Watergate case, did tell
me that, you know, they were gonna subpoena

00:14:51.629 --> 00:14:52.629
the tapes.

00:14:52.629 --> 00:14:54.319
He said, "What did you do with the tapes?"

00:14:54.319 --> 00:14:55.879
And I said, "Well, I can't tell you.

00:14:55.879 --> 00:14:56.879
You'll have to talk to our attorneys."

00:14:56.879 --> 00:14:59.620
And he said, "Well, you know, we're going
to subpoena those tapes, and if we don't,

00:14:59.620 --> 00:15:00.620
the defense will."

00:15:00.620 --> 00:15:03.339
Well, what happened was that the defense did
subpoena them.

00:15:03.339 --> 00:15:07.529
And the Justice Department, in the person
of Earl Silbert, stood up in court and said,

00:15:07.529 --> 00:15:09.660
"We have no objection to the subpoenaing of
the tapes.

00:15:09.660 --> 00:15:11.749
And as a matter of fact, we want to see them
too."

00:15:11.749 --> 00:15:17.100
And that's exactly my point, is there is intimidation,
I think, of the media when the Justice Department

00:15:17.100 --> 00:15:18.570
is involved in the subpoenaing of reporters.

00:15:18.570 --> 00:15:24.089
Edward Epstein: But let's say hypothetically
that aside from everything else, this witness,

00:15:24.089 --> 00:15:30.199
Alfred Baldwin, had already testified to the
grand jury, had told them the entire story

00:15:30.199 --> 00:15:35.670
he told you, so even if you had been intimidated
to the point that you couldn't publish your

00:15:35.670 --> 00:15:39.399
story, still the grand jury hadn't heard the
story and they would have brought it out in

00:15:39.399 --> 00:15:40.399
the trial.

00:15:40.399 --> 00:15:41.660
Jack Nelson: Well, no, that's not exactly right.

00:15:41.660 --> 00:15:42.799
Let's put it this way.

00:15:42.799 --> 00:15:47.009
In the process of investigative reporting,
this was an interview that went on over two

00:15:47.009 --> 00:15:48.009
nights.

00:15:48.009 --> 00:15:50.999
And in the process, we learned a lot of things
in that interview that did not go in the Baldwin

00:15:50.999 --> 00:15:51.999
story.

00:15:51.999 --> 00:15:53.449
It was a first-person story about Baldwin.

00:15:53.449 --> 00:15:56.759
It didn't go in because there were things
that he could not substantiate.

00:15:56.759 --> 00:15:57.759
We wanted to use these things to investigate
his leads.

00:15:57.759 --> 00:15:58.759
Do you know what happened?

00:15:58.759 --> 00:16:04.490
We sent the tapes to Los Angeles because the
editors asked us to do that because we were

00:16:04.490 --> 00:16:06.209
afraid the government were gonna get them.

00:16:06.209 --> 00:16:09.453
And we never did get access to those tapes
again, and we have yet to have access to those

00:16:09.453 --> 00:16:10.453
tapes.

00:16:10.453 --> 00:16:15.920
And there was material on that, it would have
been helpful to us at that time in investigating

00:16:15.920 --> 00:16:16.920
Watergate.

00:16:16.920 --> 00:16:20.399
Antonin Scalia: It was not the government which
ultimately subpoenaed them, though, was it?

00:16:20.399 --> 00:16:21.399
It was the defense in the case.

00:16:21.399 --> 00:16:24.239
Jack Nelson: Well, it was the defense who subpoenaed
them, but, the government well it was

00:16:24.239 --> 00:16:28.290
the government though who said that they're
going to subpoena them.

00:16:28.290 --> 00:16:31.619
And the government said, "If we don't subpoena
them, the defense will."

00:16:31.619 --> 00:16:37.000
And it was the government who stood up in
court and said, "This may raise the First

00:16:37.000 --> 00:16:38.699
Amendment question, but even if it does, the
government has no objection to it."

00:16:38.699 --> 00:16:41.470
Floyd Abrams: I'd like to come back to something
Mr. Epstein said earlier about how everyone

00:16:41.470 --> 00:16:45.790
has to testify to a crime that he's seen,
and that is certainly a true statement of

00:16:45.790 --> 00:16:46.790
the law.

00:16:46.790 --> 00:16:50.850
I don't think anyone that is advocating a
newsman's privilege is really advocating,

00:16:50.850 --> 00:16:55.910
except in one particular type case that I
could touch on, that there should be any exemption

00:16:55.910 --> 00:16:57.970
from testifying about crimes which they saw.

00:16:57.970 --> 00:16:59.290
But what people are talking about...

00:16:59.290 --> 00:17:02.139
Edward Epstein: But isn't that what he means
when he says absolute privilege?

00:17:02.139 --> 00:17:03.239
Antonin Scalia: No, he doesn't.

00:17:03.239 --> 00:17:04.780
Mr. Nelson is talking about interviewing people.

00:17:04.780 --> 00:17:06.709
Jack Nelson: Do you ever heard of the case?

00:17:06.709 --> 00:17:08.180
I mean, you know, this is always a laden argument.

00:17:08.180 --> 00:17:09.620
I've never heard... I've never heard...

00:17:11.860 --> 00:17:17.579
Antonin Scalia: Most of the Justice Department
subpoenas involve two types of situations.

00:17:17.579 --> 00:17:22.310
One of which is the exact witnessing of the
crime.

00:17:22.310 --> 00:17:26.459
Television news films of the shooting of George
Wallace, for example.

00:17:26.459 --> 00:17:30.940
And this, by the way, is one reason I think
the number of government subpoenas to newsmen

00:17:30.940 --> 00:17:38.850
has increased in recent years because of television,
because of on-the-spot coverage of events

00:17:38.850 --> 00:17:44.799
at which unlawful action occurs or is charged
to occur, such as demonstrations, Wounded

00:17:44.799 --> 00:17:46.779
Knee, and so forth and so on.

00:17:46.779 --> 00:17:52.070
These are actual films of the alleged offense.

00:17:52.070 --> 00:17:57.130
Now either the government, which is trying
to prove the offense, or even more so the

00:17:57.130 --> 00:18:00.370
defendant, who is trying to demonstrate that
he didn't do what he is charged with, it seems

00:18:00.370 --> 00:18:06.559
to me, has an overpowering right to have that
thing shown to the grand jury and to the jury

00:18:06.559 --> 00:18:07.559
if it goes to court.

00:18:07.559 --> 00:18:09.040
Floyd Abrams: That may be so in some cases.

00:18:09.040 --> 00:18:13.801
I think the problem with that, the "ABC" newsmen
were stoned in New York City when the time

00:18:13.801 --> 00:18:17.919
came in the late '60s when they were being
thought of as being police agents, an adjunct

00:18:17.919 --> 00:18:21.630
to the New York City police force because
they were being so routinely subpoenaed.

00:18:21.630 --> 00:18:27.789
That the standard crime situation in which
I think there is justification for non-testimony

00:18:27.789 --> 00:18:33.370
is the Branzburg case itself, where a reporter
goes out, investigates, finds out that hashish

00:18:33.370 --> 00:18:38.350
is being made in Louisville, Kentucky, reports
on the story, which could not have been made

00:18:38.350 --> 00:18:43.740
known to the public, absent a pledge of confidentiality,
and then is subpoenaed to testify, has to

00:18:43.740 --> 00:18:46.929
leave the state finally, else he will wind
up in jail.

00:18:46.929 --> 00:18:49.940
And I think the public is ill-served by that
kind of law.

00:18:49.940 --> 00:18:54.059
Antonin Scalia: That is not the typical case,
and I don't think that any discussion of absolute

00:18:54.059 --> 00:18:58.410
newsman's privilege should center either about
solely the government's desire to get the

00:18:58.410 --> 00:19:03.630
film or whatever it is, nor should it center
solely upon the confidential source cases,

00:19:03.630 --> 00:19:06.540
which are the most appealing from the standpoint
of the newsmen.

00:19:06.540 --> 00:19:09.520
Most of them do not involve confidential sources.

00:19:09.520 --> 00:19:17.419
The other most common category besides news
films of events is the mailing of tapes and

00:19:17.419 --> 00:19:26.309
kidnap letters and such things to newspapers
or television news stations or radio stations

00:19:26.309 --> 00:19:30.080
after a kidnapping, ransom notes, or such
and such a building is going to be blown up.

00:19:30.080 --> 00:19:34.639
Now, on occasion, these have been denied when
the police wanted the note for the purpose

00:19:34.639 --> 00:19:39.580
of trying to detect form it where the individuals
concerned happen to be located.

00:19:39.580 --> 00:19:43.039
I see no reason for the extension of a privilege
in such cases.

00:19:43.039 --> 00:19:46.880
There's no confidentiality involved whatever,
and ordinarily there isn't.

00:19:46.880 --> 00:19:51.799
William Ruckelshaus: Have you ever been chilled
by a government subpoena that you received?

00:19:51.799 --> 00:19:53.080
Charles Seib: No, never.

00:19:53.080 --> 00:19:58.789
When I was managing editor at the "Star",
we had the Agnew incident where there were

00:19:58.789 --> 00:20:04.120
some effort to get our reporter's notes and
that died.

00:20:04.120 --> 00:20:12.039
I haven't, but I was wondering, we talk about
the increase in the number of subpoenas and

00:20:12.039 --> 00:20:14.889
legal efforts to get material from the press.

00:20:14.889 --> 00:20:21.940
Is any of that due to the sort of separation
between the press and the authorities in the

00:20:21.940 --> 00:20:27.740
government that the things that used to be
done cooperatively between the press and the

00:20:27.740 --> 00:20:35.059
authorities is now at the arm's length distance
between the two has widened and we're more

00:20:35.059 --> 00:20:37.150
conscious of the need for separation?

00:20:37.150 --> 00:20:39.610
Is that one of the factors here?

00:20:39.610 --> 00:20:42.270
Antonin Scalia: You gentlemen would know better
than I.

00:20:42.270 --> 00:20:43.640
Jack Nelson: I wouldn't think so.

00:20:43.640 --> 00:20:45.870
I mean, it may be a minor factor, but I wouldn't
think so.

00:20:45.870 --> 00:20:52.860
I mean, for example, newspaper reporters are
cooperating all the time in cases involving

00:20:52.860 --> 00:20:57.820
congressional investigations, in cases involving
Justice Department or police investigations.

00:20:57.820 --> 00:21:02.100
It's not unusual for a reporter to learn of
some information that has to do with a crime

00:21:02.100 --> 00:21:04.730
and to cooperate with law enforcement officers.

00:21:04.730 --> 00:21:09.039
That's the reason I think that the argument
about whether a reporter will testify if he

00:21:09.039 --> 00:21:12.730
witnessed a crime, I don't know of any cases
where reporters really witnessed crimes.

00:21:12.730 --> 00:21:16.760
Unless as you say, in the case where they
went out and asked somebody to let them see

00:21:16.760 --> 00:21:20.090
them make hashish so that they could do a
documentary on it.

00:21:20.090 --> 00:21:22.390
In which case, nobody would have known of
the crime anyway.

00:21:22.390 --> 00:21:26.289
You raised the case when you were testifying,
for example, a hypothetical situation on the

00:21:26.289 --> 00:21:27.630
Hill about the shield law.

00:21:27.630 --> 00:21:33.020
And you said that there could be a situation
now, if you had an absolute shield law, where

00:21:33.020 --> 00:21:38.900
a television station could have an interview
with Patty Hearst, and then the Justice Department

00:21:38.900 --> 00:21:43.919
or other law enforcement officials would be
unable to compel the television station to

00:21:43.919 --> 00:21:45.990
say where they got Patty Hearst, and so forth.

00:21:45.990 --> 00:21:50.779
Well, the point is that you would know a hell
of a lot more about Patty Hearst at that space

00:21:50.779 --> 00:21:53.059
than you did before if they didn't have the
interview.

00:21:53.059 --> 00:21:57.230
And had Patty Hearst known that this television
station would tell the Justice Department

00:21:57.230 --> 00:22:01.019
or anybody else where she was, she would never
have shown up for the interview.

00:22:01.019 --> 00:22:02.020
And this happens all the time.

00:22:02.020 --> 00:22:06.950
Antonin Scalia: The point I was making at that
point in my testimony was a quite different

00:22:06.950 --> 00:22:10.090
point from the one we have just been discussing.

00:22:10.090 --> 00:22:14.370
I think that situation could occur if you
had an absolute shield law.

00:22:14.370 --> 00:22:20.559
And the main reason I think that situation
would be bad is how seriously does a society

00:22:20.559 --> 00:22:27.660
really take the battle against crime if it
could allow something like this to happen.

00:22:27.660 --> 00:22:31.029
You know, I don't know where Patty Hearst
is on the FBI's most-wanted list, but let's

00:22:31.029 --> 00:22:32.549
assume she's number one.

00:22:32.549 --> 00:22:38.470
And there she is, big as life on the screen,
in living color, and, you know, a news reporter

00:22:38.470 --> 00:22:39.470
having an interview with her.

00:22:39.470 --> 00:22:43.590
I just don't think a society can take its
law enforcement efforts seriously if it can

00:22:43.590 --> 00:22:44.830
allow something like that to occur.

00:22:44.830 --> 00:22:49.600
Floyd Abrams: We've done that in 24 states where
we have privilege laws, many of which are

00:22:49.600 --> 00:22:50.600
absolute.

00:22:50.600 --> 00:22:53.340
In New York, for example, that that interview
did occur with Patty Hearst, the New York

00:22:53.340 --> 00:22:57.220
state authorities could not require a newsman
to disclose the source of that.

00:22:57.220 --> 00:22:59.179
William Ruckelshaus: I think Mr. Scalia is saying
he doesn't agree with those things.

00:22:59.179 --> 00:23:00.179
Antonin Scalia: I am sad for New York.

00:23:00.179 --> 00:23:05.740
Floyd Abrams: I think I understand that, but
what I'm saying is that the country hasn't

00:23:05.740 --> 00:23:07.190
exactly collapsed either.

00:23:07.190 --> 00:23:11.450
New York is not precisely the best example
of the best functioning.

00:23:11.450 --> 00:23:16.790
But, you know, we do have these statutes around
the country.

00:23:16.790 --> 00:23:21.710
We've seen no examples that I can cite, or
that have been cited in testimony that I've

00:23:21.710 --> 00:23:22.710
read.

00:23:22.710 --> 00:23:23.710
Antonin Scalia: Well, maybe New York doesn't have
Patty Hearst.

00:23:23.710 --> 00:23:24.710
I don't know.

00:23:24.710 --> 00:23:25.710
I think...

00:23:25.710 --> 00:23:26.710
Floyd Abrams: New York has Abbie Hoffman.

00:23:26.710 --> 00:23:28.850
Antonin Scalia: I think the Patty Hearst thing
would happen, if Patty Hearst would offer

00:23:28.850 --> 00:23:30.950
it, there's somebody who would snap it up
like that.

00:23:30.950 --> 00:23:35.529
And I think that something like that would
just have an enormously corrosive effect upon

00:23:35.529 --> 00:23:38.820
not just law enforcement but the entire society's
respect for the law.

00:23:38.820 --> 00:23:41.350
Jack Nelson: Wouldn't the FBI and the Justice
Department know a lot more about Patty Hearst

00:23:41.350 --> 00:23:43.890
if they saw her interviewed on television
than they know right now?

00:23:43.890 --> 00:23:44.940
I mean, they know very little now.

00:23:44.940 --> 00:23:46.440
Antonin Scalia: No, not necessarily.

00:23:46.440 --> 00:23:49.580
I guess they'd have a current photograph of
her that might be helpful.

00:23:49.580 --> 00:23:52.440
Edward Epstein: But she might be disguised.

00:23:52.440 --> 00:23:56.889
Antonin Scalia: You're seriously asserting that
this would be a help to law enforcement efforts?

00:23:56.889 --> 00:23:59.340
Jack Nelson: Well, I don't know, but I'm not
saying it wouldn't be a hindrance.

00:23:59.340 --> 00:24:03.450
Floyd Abrams: I would seriously assert that
it's a help to the public to be able to watch

00:24:03.450 --> 00:24:05.740
an interview with Patty Hearst rather than
not.

00:24:05.740 --> 00:24:09.360
And the effect of what you're saying is that,
in the future, at least, you couldn't.

00:24:09.360 --> 00:24:10.960
Antonin Scalia: Well, what if it was Jack the
Ripper?

00:24:10.960 --> 00:24:11.960
Would that make any difference?

00:24:11.960 --> 00:24:16.039
I mean here is Jack the Ripper in living color,
you know, "Hi, out there.

00:24:16.039 --> 00:24:17.190
I'm being interviewing on television.

00:24:17.190 --> 00:24:20.889
I know the police can't get this film and
they can't know where I'm being interviewed,

00:24:20.889 --> 00:24:24.440
because the laws we live under say it's more
important to watch me than it is to catch

00:24:24.440 --> 00:24:25.440
me."

00:24:25.440 --> 00:24:33.279
Jack Nelson: Suppose it was Adolf Hitler when
he was alive.

00:24:33.279 --> 00:24:34.960
William Ruckelshaus: Mr. Epstein has something.

00:24:34.960 --> 00:24:40.509
Edward Epstein: Now Jack, when you said witness
a crime, that you would be for a witness being

00:24:40.509 --> 00:24:45.529
compelled to testify if he witnesses a crime,
what happened is you interviewed someone or

00:24:45.529 --> 00:24:50.259
another reporter you respected interviewed
someone who said in the course of the interview

00:24:50.259 --> 00:24:54.659
or inferred that he might assassinate the
President?

00:24:54.659 --> 00:24:56.730
And this came out in the interview...

00:24:56.730 --> 00:24:58.570
Jack Nelson: Well, obviously, I'd go directly
to the authorities.

00:24:58.570 --> 00:25:00.880
Edward Epstein: No, but let's say this came out
in the interview.

00:25:00.880 --> 00:25:04.809
You put it in your story, and then your notes
would testify to see if they could build a

00:25:04.809 --> 00:25:07.720
case against the person who said this.

00:25:07.720 --> 00:25:08.970
Would you submit your notes?

00:25:08.970 --> 00:25:13.900
Would you testify to the portions of the interview
that you didn't, because this was the Caldwell

00:25:13.900 --> 00:25:14.900
case?

00:25:14.900 --> 00:25:18.020
Jack Nelson: No, how is that in the Caldwell
case?

00:25:18.020 --> 00:25:19.230
That's not the Caldwell case at all.

00:25:19.230 --> 00:25:20.600
William Ruckelshaus: Well, let's not argue about
this.

00:25:20.600 --> 00:25:22.210
Let's take it as a hypothetical.

00:25:22.210 --> 00:25:23.269
What would you do?

00:25:23.269 --> 00:25:25.600
Jack Nelson: Well, I'd go directly to the authorities.

00:25:25.600 --> 00:25:29.380
I don't think I would need to...if somebody
told me they were getting ready to assassinate

00:25:29.380 --> 00:25:30.919
the president or anybody else.

00:25:30.919 --> 00:25:33.880
And see, that's the reason I say this is a
false issue.

00:25:33.880 --> 00:25:38.269
I don't think any reporter who's a human being
would listen to someone threatening, say they're

00:25:38.269 --> 00:25:41.340
gonna kill someone else, no matter who it
is and not take action between...

00:25:41.340 --> 00:25:42.240
Antonin Scalia: What happens then if the government
subpoenas you on that one?

00:25:42.240 --> 00:25:43.590
Edward Epstein: How do you define it in court?

00:25:43.590 --> 00:25:46.870
Jack Nelson: I define anybody who's gathering
the news for public consumption.

00:25:46.870 --> 00:25:50.559
And that's the reason I think the unqualified
shield law would not just apply to me, but

00:25:50.559 --> 00:25:54.679
it would apply to anybody who went down to
city hall, got information from a confidential

00:25:54.679 --> 00:25:56.639
source, came out, and distributed it.

00:25:56.639 --> 00:25:57.639
I think there ought to be an unqualified shield
law.

00:25:57.639 --> 00:26:01.960
Floyd Abrams: I think we have to decide which
examples are more real, and I really think

00:26:01.960 --> 00:26:05.440
that a good part of the resolution of these
things has got to go on that.

00:26:05.440 --> 00:26:11.690
You do have a nice case, I think, Ed, on the
presidential assassin, in the abstract, and

00:26:11.690 --> 00:26:12.890
Jack the Ripper in the abstract.

00:26:12.890 --> 00:26:19.080
I think that there's a more real case, and
that reality is really Paul Branzburg.

00:26:19.080 --> 00:26:23.370
And that reality is really a reporter who
goes down to a police station, interviews

00:26:23.370 --> 00:26:27.070
a policeman, and is then made a witness to
his own interview with the policeman.

00:26:27.070 --> 00:26:32.059
And I had just been through that with a reporter
from the New York Times, a witness to his

00:26:32.059 --> 00:26:36.950
own interview with a policeman, some of which
was confidential, some of which was not.

00:26:36.950 --> 00:26:41.809
In totality, it can only interfere with the
public getting the kind of information I think

00:26:41.809 --> 00:26:43.000
it should get.

00:26:43.000 --> 00:26:47.519
I really don't think that the assassination
example, which lawyers, incidentally, are

00:26:47.519 --> 00:26:51.500
confronted with in court every time that they
argue these cases...

00:26:51.500 --> 00:26:53.830
Antonin Scalia: Was this the prosecution or the
defense?

00:26:53.830 --> 00:26:54.960
Floyd Abrams: In that case, it was the defense.

00:26:54.960 --> 00:26:57.169
Antonin Scalia: It wasn't often is.

00:26:57.169 --> 00:27:03.570
And if I were the defendant, I wouldn't feel
so horrified about, in this instance, maybe

00:27:03.570 --> 00:27:06.770
not giving the newsman as much leeway as he
would like.

00:27:06.770 --> 00:27:11.010
It's a very hard case when you're talking
about sending a man to jail or not.

00:27:11.010 --> 00:27:15.659
Floyd Abrams: Yeah, but that doesn't put any
First Amendment input on the scale at all.

00:27:15.659 --> 00:27:16.659
Sure it's a hard case.

00:27:16.659 --> 00:27:18.539
There are a lot of hard cases.

00:27:18.539 --> 00:27:22.330
And the fact that it's a defendant, in fact,
does impress me more than the fact that it's

00:27:22.330 --> 00:27:23.330
law enforcement.

00:27:23.330 --> 00:27:25.269
But to say that it doesn't seem to me answers
it.

00:27:25.269 --> 00:27:26.331
Antonin Scalia: No, it doesn't answer it.

00:27:26.331 --> 00:27:29.970
And I concede that a court should examine
each case in which a subpoena is sought and

00:27:29.970 --> 00:27:33.120
say, "Is this really necessary to the case?

00:27:33.120 --> 00:27:35.870
Is it being done for the purpose of harassment?"
and so forth and so on.

00:27:35.870 --> 00:27:39.389
But that is something quite different from
laying out either a categorical shield law

00:27:39.389 --> 00:27:43.809
or even a qualified law that gives an absolute
privilege in certain defined cases, which

00:27:43.809 --> 00:27:45.250
I find is very difficult to define.

00:27:45.250 --> 00:27:50.749
William Ruckelshaus: Do you think, Mr. Abrams,
that there is a right to know on the part

00:27:50.749 --> 00:27:51.830
of the public?

00:27:51.830 --> 00:27:57.659
Floyd Abrams: Let me say that that's a phrase
that troubles me because if there is a right

00:27:57.659 --> 00:28:03.309
to know, then I suppose the public has a right
to compel the press to print things or the

00:28:03.309 --> 00:28:08.809
judiciary may have the right or someone may
have the right to decide what it is the public

00:28:08.809 --> 00:28:09.809
should know.

00:28:09.809 --> 00:28:14.860
I think that the First Amendment ultimately
serves the public by letting it learn things,

00:28:14.860 --> 00:28:19.890
letting it hear things on television, letting
it see things that it wouldn't otherwise know.

00:28:19.890 --> 00:28:24.289
There was a case out in Indiana last year
involving "ABC" in which they were barred

00:28:24.289 --> 00:28:30.580
by a prior restraint, an injunction by a court,
for over 200 days from doing a show demonstrating,

00:28:30.580 --> 00:28:35.550
proving, that plastic cribs burned more quickly
than wooden cribs.

00:28:35.550 --> 00:28:41.370
I think the public had the right to know precisely
what "ABC" wanted to tell them, and that that

00:28:41.370 --> 00:28:45.549
it was the most offensive interference with
the public's right and the press's rights

00:28:45.549 --> 00:28:50.960
for the courts to intervene in that area and
to interject an injunction which prevented

00:28:50.960 --> 00:28:52.789
the public from knowing what was going on
there.

00:28:52.789 --> 00:29:00.230
William Ruckelshaus: What about the case of the
producer of hashish and the impact that that

00:29:00.230 --> 00:29:05.289
production might have not only on the public
but the children or people watching that television

00:29:05.289 --> 00:29:06.909
program?

00:29:06.909 --> 00:29:12.770
Does the society have some very strong interest
in revealing who it is that is producing that

00:29:12.770 --> 00:29:16.059
and likely to introduce it into the market?

00:29:16.059 --> 00:29:19.130
Jack Nelson: That was not the thrust of that
particular program, though, was it?

00:29:19.130 --> 00:29:23.059
Floyd Abrams: In the Branzburg case, the thrust
of it was how easy it was to make hashish

00:29:23.059 --> 00:29:24.059
in Louisville.

00:29:24.059 --> 00:29:25.059
Jack Nelson: Yes.

00:29:25.059 --> 00:29:28.094
It was not to show people how to do it or
to encourage its use.

00:29:28.094 --> 00:29:31.149
William Ruckelshaus: It showed somebody doing
it though who would then presumably distribute

00:29:31.149 --> 00:29:32.419
it in the community.

00:29:32.419 --> 00:29:33.470
Jack Nelson: That's right.

00:29:33.470 --> 00:29:38.149
But the point of it though was not to promote
the use of it but to discourage it.

00:29:38.149 --> 00:29:41.540
That was the point of the program, was it
not?

00:29:41.540 --> 00:29:42.790
Antonin Scalia: Yes.

00:29:42.790 --> 00:29:45.100
That goes back to my Patty Hearst case.

00:29:45.100 --> 00:29:49.640
I don't like that kind of a show because it
again is telling the society, "Look at what

00:29:49.640 --> 00:29:50.640
you can do.

00:29:50.640 --> 00:29:51.640
You can go and take a picture of this thing."

00:29:51.640 --> 00:29:54.990
And somebody said, "Gee, why don't the cops get those guys that are

00:29:54.990 --> 00:29:58.240
making up this dope and, you know, it's gonna
go out on the streets, but, well, we will

00:29:58.240 --> 00:29:59.240
not do anything.

00:29:59.240 --> 00:30:00.240
Floyd Abrams: I know you don't like that kind
of show and...

00:30:00.240 --> 00:30:05.120
Antonin Scalia: I think it's outrageous, and it
has to corrode the whole attitude of the society

00:30:05.120 --> 00:30:07.139
towards whether it is really opposed to crime.

00:30:07.139 --> 00:30:11.799
Floyd Abrams: But I really think that the Department
of Justice or the courts should refrain very

00:30:11.799 --> 00:30:17.279
strongly from imposing what they don't like,
which is what you're talking about, into law.

00:30:17.279 --> 00:30:21.850
It may be, and indeed it is true, a lot of
things the press prints are harmful.

00:30:21.850 --> 00:30:24.429
They're destructive, they hurt people.

00:30:24.429 --> 00:30:28.749
I think we've made a decision in the First
Amendment to run that risk, and not to run

00:30:28.749 --> 00:30:31.639
the risk of having government officials make
those decisions.

00:30:31.639 --> 00:30:33.909
William Ruckelshaus: How great should that risk
be?

00:30:33.909 --> 00:30:40.880
We might move into the issue of libel laws.

00:30:40.880 --> 00:30:45.970
Is there any right that should accrue to a
private citizen to sue the press if they step

00:30:45.970 --> 00:30:49.420
over certain bounds in accusing them of wrong?

00:30:49.420 --> 00:30:51.460
Jack Nelson: Well, they have that
right, don't they?

00:30:51.460 --> 00:30:53.180
Yes, they have the right under libel laws.

00:30:53.183 --> 00:30:54.183
William Ruckelshaus: Yes, they do.

00:30:54.183 --> 00:30:56.799
I wonder whether you agree that they should
under your theory of absolute...

00:30:56.799 --> 00:31:01.429
Jack Nelson: Well, under my theory of absolute
privilege I'm not so sure that I'm too much

00:31:01.429 --> 00:31:05.809
in favor of libel laws, I have to admit.

00:31:05.809 --> 00:31:07.200
But they do have the right.

00:31:07.200 --> 00:31:08.200
William Ruckelshaus: Unfortunately?

00:31:08.200 --> 00:31:09.690
Jack Nelson: Yeah, unfortunately, they do have
the right.

00:31:09.690 --> 00:31:15.371
You know, I'm almost in favor of absolute
complete press freedom, whatever that may

00:31:15.371 --> 00:31:16.371
be.

00:31:16.371 --> 00:31:19.769
Edward Epstein: Then it raises the issue of whether
the public would get the truth or a falsity,

00:31:19.769 --> 00:31:25.049
because even if the reporters had the right
to publish anything and to conceal their sources

00:31:25.049 --> 00:31:30.960
so they could concoct any deep source they
wanted, it's not clear that there'd be any

00:31:30.960 --> 00:31:34.899
way of testing the truth of what they were
publishing or that, in the long run, newspapers

00:31:34.900 --> 00:31:36.320
would have any credibility.

00:31:36.320 --> 00:31:40.920
Jack Nelson: Well, they're a part of the free
enterprise system, and they sink or swim eventually

00:31:40.929 --> 00:31:41.929
on their credibility.

00:31:41.929 --> 00:31:46.730
Edward Epstein: But the newspapers that succeed
with the public might succeed because they

00:31:46.730 --> 00:31:50.070
publish very plausible lies or unbelievable
truths.

00:31:50.070 --> 00:31:51.070
Jack Nelson: Some of them do.

00:31:51.070 --> 00:31:52.070
Edward Epstein: Right, and that's what you would
want to see?

00:31:52.070 --> 00:31:55.539
Mr. Nelson: Well, that's not what I want to
see, it's what happens in this free market,

00:31:55.539 --> 00:31:57.129
in this market of free ideas.

00:31:57.129 --> 00:32:00.840
And I don't see it as all bad, it's not all
good but it's not all bad.

00:32:00.840 --> 00:32:01.840
Edward Epstein: There's an alternative

00:32:01.840 --> 00:32:04.080
Jack Nelson: Yeah, I think the alternative
is government regulation.

00:32:04.080 --> 00:32:05.630
Edward Epstein: Well, to have strong libel laws.

00:32:05.630 --> 00:32:07.179
No, no, have strong libel laws.

00:32:07.179 --> 00:32:08.179
Jack Nelson: Well, that's government regulation.

00:32:08.179 --> 00:32:12.570
Edward Epstein: And to have...just this happens
in any book publishing or academic where...

00:32:12.570 --> 00:32:15.040
Jack Nelson: You're in favor of stronger libel
laws than we now have?

00:32:15.040 --> 00:32:20.200
Edward Epstein: Oh, the libel laws we now have
are a joke when it applies to a public figure.

00:32:20.200 --> 00:32:26.249
It's almost impossible for a public figure
to successfully sue and it's very convenient

00:32:26.249 --> 00:32:27.559
for me, as a journalist.

00:32:27.559 --> 00:32:30.510
I mean, it makes things very easy.

00:32:30.510 --> 00:32:34.179
But I think that what's going to happen in
the long run is the press is going to lose

00:32:34.179 --> 00:32:38.620
its credibility because so many things will
be published that no one will know what to

00:32:38.620 --> 00:32:39.620
believe.

00:32:39.620 --> 00:32:42.559
Jack Nelson: Well, Mr. Seib is part of the
effort to help us restore that credibility

00:32:42.559 --> 00:32:44.850
by being the ombudsman for the "Washington
Post".

00:32:44.850 --> 00:32:46.769
William Ruckelshaus: What is an ombudsman, Mr.
Seib?

00:32:46.769 --> 00:32:47.769
Charles Seib: Good question.

00:32:47.769 --> 00:32:48.769
William Ruckelshaus: Tell us.

00:32:48.769 --> 00:32:50.399
I think it is a growing trend.

00:32:50.399 --> 00:32:51.679
Charles Seib: That's a growing trend.

00:32:51.679 --> 00:32:55.549
The press trying to police itself, I don't
know if it's gonna work or not.

00:32:55.549 --> 00:33:01.049
I've been in this job eight months now, and
I'm not sure whether I'm doing good or harm.

00:33:01.049 --> 00:33:10.039
But until the press is ready to accept its
responsibilities a little better than I think

00:33:10.039 --> 00:33:15.850
it has that it does generally, I think probably
the libel laws, at least protecting the rights

00:33:15.850 --> 00:33:23.710
of the private citizen who doesn't by running
for office or by taking public office expose

00:33:23.710 --> 00:33:30.299
himself, I think that's the minimum we should
have right now.

00:33:30.299 --> 00:33:36.270
I can't quite see that the press is ready
to remove all restraints and rely totally

00:33:36.270 --> 00:33:44.210
on the marketplace and the ultimate triumph
of good over evil to solve the problem.

00:33:44.210 --> 00:33:52.160
The area I'm concerned with is fairness and
accuracy and balance that doesn't really come

00:33:52.160 --> 00:33:53.200
under the libel laws.

00:33:53.200 --> 00:33:58.679
The libel laws are sort of a last resort for
the, as you say, the public figure really

00:33:58.679 --> 00:34:00.509
doesn't have much access to them anymore.

00:34:00.509 --> 00:34:02.000
Jack Nelson: Well, you're the access.

00:34:02.000 --> 00:34:09.490
Charles Seib: I'm the access now, to the extent
that a corrective piece or a discussion piece

00:34:09.490 --> 00:34:11.399
ever catches up with the original error.

00:34:11.399 --> 00:34:14.550
William Ruckelshaus: Let me try to get back to
that towards the end, Mr. Seib, because I

00:34:14.550 --> 00:34:20.960
think it's important what might be done by
the press itself to ensure responsibility.

00:34:20.960 --> 00:34:26.800
Do you see any other restrictions, for instance,
in the name of national security on the right

00:34:26.800 --> 00:34:30.040
of the press to print material that comes
into their hands?

00:34:30.040 --> 00:34:38.700
Charles Seib: I don't really have...I'm sure you
could concoct a case where national security

00:34:38.700 --> 00:34:46.580
or the security of certain individuals required
restraint in publishing a story, at least

00:34:46.580 --> 00:34:49.310
for a short time.

00:34:49.310 --> 00:34:55.620
But generally speaking, the claims to national
security I think should be very harshly examined

00:34:55.620 --> 00:34:58.200
by the editors, by the press.

00:34:58.200 --> 00:35:02.960
My position is that the press does have an
absolute privilege under the First Amendment,

00:35:02.960 --> 00:35:04.560
that editors be...

00:35:04.560 --> 00:35:06.860
Edward Epstein: Absolute privilege to do what?

00:35:06.860 --> 00:35:09.510
Charles Seib: To report whatever it gets a hold
of.

00:35:09.510 --> 00:35:15.440
William Ruckelshaus: That the courts, in other
words, have no role in trying to restrict

00:35:15.440 --> 00:35:22.070
the question in printing, even if were to
involve what by a reasonable man's definition

00:35:22.070 --> 00:35:28.340
would be a grossly irresponsible act, may
even jeopardize lives of some people, still

00:35:28.340 --> 00:35:30.760
the courts would have no right to move in.

00:35:30.760 --> 00:35:33.000
Charles Seib: Well, that's hard to say.

00:35:33.000 --> 00:35:37.580
I mean, philosophically I believe that because
I believe that...

00:35:37.580 --> 00:35:38.580
William Ruckelshaus: How about in practice?

00:35:38.580 --> 00:35:42.380
Charles Seib: In practice, first of all, I think
they would move in.

00:35:42.380 --> 00:35:44.930
I think it's kind of a...

00:35:44.930 --> 00:35:48.980
Jack Nelson: Look, it's not very hard to get
the press not to run a story.

00:35:48.980 --> 00:35:49.980
Charles Seib: That's right.

00:35:49.980 --> 00:35:50.980
It's too easy, as a matter of fact.

00:35:50.980 --> 00:35:54.220
Jack Nelson: It's very easy to get the press
not to run a story.

00:35:54.220 --> 00:35:57.910
William Colby who went around and got the
"Los Angeles Times" and the "New York Times"

00:35:57.910 --> 00:36:02.560
and the "Washington Post" and "Newsweek" and
"Time" and "NBC" and "CBS" and "ABC" and Public

00:36:02.560 --> 00:36:06.830
Broadcasting, and I don't know who else, not
to run the Glomar Explorer story on the grounds

00:36:06.830 --> 00:36:07.830
of national security.

00:36:07.830 --> 00:36:11.950
Now, the story ran, and nobody's been able
to tell anyone where national security was

00:36:11.950 --> 00:36:12.950
really involved in that.

00:36:12.950 --> 00:36:20.160
Charles Seib: The picture of this official running
around town, trying to stop this thing, dribbling

00:36:20.160 --> 00:36:27.090
little bits of information as he went, because
in each conversation he, in order to convince

00:36:27.090 --> 00:36:31.770
the parties not to publish, he gave them a
little more information.

00:36:31.770 --> 00:36:33.122
It was really ridiculous when it came down to it.

00:36:33.122 --> 00:36:35.540
Edward Epstein: But do you think that the public
interest was served?

00:36:35.540 --> 00:36:38.750
Actually, I read the story in the "Los Angeles
Times".

00:36:38.750 --> 00:36:41.700
Didn't they publish the story, before the
times, two weeks before?

00:36:41.700 --> 00:36:42.700
Charles Seib: They had the wrong ocean.

00:36:42.700 --> 00:36:46.040
Jack Nelson: Well, that was before Mr. Colby
had...

00:36:46.040 --> 00:36:47.170
Edward Epstein: Right.

00:36:47.170 --> 00:36:48.880
So the story was already published.

00:36:48.880 --> 00:36:53.430
But, aside from that, I don't see the public
interest that was served by publishing that

00:36:53.430 --> 00:36:58.420
the CIA was perfectly legitimately retrieving
a Russian submarine from international waters.

00:36:58.420 --> 00:37:03.220
That's the only legitimate operation I ever
heard of that the CIA has done.

00:37:03.220 --> 00:37:04.500
Jack Nelson: Let me ask you something.

00:37:04.520 --> 00:37:10.600
Here was a ship out in the middle of the ocean,
operated by Howard Hughes, who's always been...

00:37:10.600 --> 00:37:12.510
Edward Epstein: It wasn't operated by Howard
Hughes.

00:37:12.510 --> 00:37:13.700
Jack Nelson: Well, all right.

00:37:13.700 --> 00:37:14.770
It was owned by Howard Hughes.

00:37:14.770 --> 00:37:16.150
It was a Howard Hughes operation.

00:37:16.150 --> 00:37:17.150
It was a Howard Hughes-CIA operation.

00:37:17.150 --> 00:37:18.150
Sure it was.

00:37:18.150 --> 00:37:19.150
The CIA itself says it was.

00:37:19.150 --> 00:37:20.430
Edward Epstein: It was a CIA operation.

00:37:20.430 --> 00:37:22.300
Jack Nelson: It was a Howard Hughes-CIA operation.

00:37:22.300 --> 00:37:28.430
In any event, do you see nothing wrong with
a private contractor out on the sea recovering

00:37:28.430 --> 00:37:36.560
a Russian submarine, and with the involvement
of Howard Hughes who's always been on sort

00:37:36.560 --> 00:37:39.340
of the fringes of shady government operations?

00:37:39.340 --> 00:37:40.360
You see nothing wrong with that?

00:37:40.360 --> 00:37:43.340
And you don't think the American people are
entitled to know about that?

00:37:43.340 --> 00:37:45.440
Edward Epstein: Wait a moment.

00:37:45.440 --> 00:37:51.720
The retrieval of the submarine was done by...Howard
Hughes built the ship, but then it was taken

00:37:51.720 --> 00:37:53.910
over by another company, Glomar.

00:37:53.910 --> 00:37:57.570
Jack Nelson: Yeah, which is also a Howard Hughes
operation.

00:37:57.570 --> 00:37:59.240
Edward Epstein: Well, in any case, I think it
was...

00:37:59.240 --> 00:38:01.330
Antonin Scalia: Listen, I'll say no to his question
if nobody else will say no.

00:38:01.330 --> 00:38:02.330
I don't see anything wrong with it.

00:38:02.330 --> 00:38:04.940
I think it was a great job, if indeed it happened
the way they said and...

00:38:04.940 --> 00:38:08.780
William Ruckelshaus: Well, why not publish it?

00:38:08.780 --> 00:38:13.000
Edward Epstein: Well, the reason not to publish
it is very clear, is it stopped them from

00:38:13.000 --> 00:38:14.310
retrieving the rest of the submarine.

00:38:14.310 --> 00:38:16.230
That's the reason not to publish it and it...

00:38:16.230 --> 00:38:17.740
Floyd Abrams: That's what they say.

00:38:17.740 --> 00:38:19.500
Edward Epstein: ...it destroys the expenditures.

00:38:19.500 --> 00:38:22.580
Jack Nelson: Do you think that the Russians
didn't know that the ship was out there?

00:38:22.580 --> 00:38:24.732
I mean the CIA said they assumed that they
knew.

00:38:24.732 --> 00:38:25.820
Edward Epstein: But this is the key issue.

00:38:25.820 --> 00:38:27.860
Who is to be the arbiter of what...

00:38:27.860 --> 00:38:29.150
Jack Nelson: I would say the press.

00:38:29.150 --> 00:38:30.640
Edward Epstein: You want the press to be the
arbiter?

00:38:30.640 --> 00:38:35.430
Let's say something is of interest to the
national security where the press would somehow

00:38:35.430 --> 00:38:38.040
know what the Russians knew and what the Russians
didn't know?

00:38:38.040 --> 00:38:39.040
I don't want to be the arbiter of what...

00:38:39.040 --> 00:38:42.030
Jack Nelson: Listen, they claim national security
argument was used to cover up Watergate, to

00:38:42.030 --> 00:38:43.950
cover up the Ellsberg burglary.

00:38:43.950 --> 00:38:48.010
All of the things that came out after Watergate,
national security was stamped on almost every

00:38:48.010 --> 00:38:49.010
one of them.

00:38:49.010 --> 00:38:52.080
Edward Epstein: Right, of course, in other words,
you want to be the arbiter of whether some

00:38:52.080 --> 00:38:53.440
operation is a national security or not.

00:38:53.440 --> 00:38:54.440
Let's just get this very clear.

00:38:54.440 --> 00:38:55.440
Jack Nelson: Well, let's face it.

00:38:55.440 --> 00:38:56.440
Yeah, I think the press should be.

00:38:56.440 --> 00:38:59.750
Edward Epstein: And you feel that your education
and your knowledge of the world that you are

00:38:59.750 --> 00:39:00.750
someone...

00:39:00.750 --> 00:39:01.750
Jack Nelson: Not just me, the press generally.

00:39:01.750 --> 00:39:04.380
Edward Epstein: Well, I just can't take that presumption.

00:39:04.380 --> 00:39:09.300
Floyd Abrams: Mr. Epstein, the alternative is
allowing the Department of Justice and the

00:39:09.310 --> 00:39:13.960
court system, that other branch of government,
to make those same decisions as to what the

00:39:13.960 --> 00:39:15.600
press can print.

00:39:15.600 --> 00:39:19.171
And I think that that has very considerable
risks, which are well recognized in the First

00:39:19.171 --> 00:39:20.171
Amendment itself.

00:39:20.171 --> 00:39:24.010
The Pentagon Papers, of course, is a good
example of that.

00:39:24.010 --> 00:39:29.120
God knows we heard enough about national security
in that case, about documents which were historical

00:39:29.120 --> 00:39:30.120
in nature...

00:39:30.120 --> 00:39:35.110
Jack Nelson: But that was another case of where
the press cooperated with the government and

00:39:35.110 --> 00:39:36.110
did not run them.

00:39:36.110 --> 00:39:37.110
Antonin Scalia: Wait a minute, who do you mean
by the press?

00:39:37.110 --> 00:39:38.730
I asked before who was the newsman.

00:39:38.730 --> 00:39:41.900
And you're talking about the press cooperating,
when we get the picture everybody would, you

00:39:41.900 --> 00:39:42.900
know?

00:39:42.900 --> 00:39:46.290
Edward Epstein: Well, the "New York Times", they'd
run the Pentagon Papers until it was authorized

00:39:46.290 --> 00:39:47.990
by the court, isn't that right?

00:39:47.990 --> 00:39:52.460
Antonin Scalia: Some cases that have come before
the court have involved reporters from an

00:39:52.460 --> 00:39:57.740
SDS underground newspaper, and do these people
have any less claim to being reporters?

00:39:57.740 --> 00:39:58.740
Jack Nelson: No.

00:39:58.740 --> 00:40:05.570
Antonin Scalia: All right, then you say it's one
thing if a spy gets information and disseminates

00:40:05.570 --> 00:40:09.080
it, but it's quite something else if he's
working on the staff of the "Daily Worker".

00:40:09.080 --> 00:40:11.540
Is that the distinction you draw?

00:40:11.540 --> 00:40:13.660
Floyd Abrams: If it's the "Daily Worker", it
may be espionage.

00:40:13.660 --> 00:40:17.480
And if it's espionage, it should be liable
to criminal prosecution.

00:40:17.480 --> 00:40:23.610
But if it's news gathering, I think there
is a distinction.

00:40:23.610 --> 00:40:25.240
I don't think it's a hard distinction.

00:40:25.240 --> 00:40:28.700
Antonin Scalia: I don't want to be on the other
side in court, having to demonstrate that

00:40:28.700 --> 00:40:29.700
distinction.

00:40:29.700 --> 00:40:34.060
Floyd Abrams: Well, I would hope that you would
recognize that distinction between the "Daily

00:40:34.060 --> 00:40:39.550
Worker" and other newspapers which gather
news for the purpose of reporting news to

00:40:39.550 --> 00:40:40.550
the public.

00:40:40.550 --> 00:40:42.800
Antonin Scalia: Well, you surely can't distinguish
between one newspaper and another.

00:40:42.800 --> 00:40:45.870
You must have some principle as to how it's
acquired or why it's acquired.

00:40:45.870 --> 00:40:50.750
Jack Nelson: Look, you know if a reporter for
the "Daily Worker" is involved in espionage

00:40:50.750 --> 00:40:54.090
work, the results of that are not gonna show
up in the "Daily Worker".

00:40:54.090 --> 00:41:00.040
Charles Seib: You have to assume that to have
a free press, and I'm convinced that the system

00:41:00.040 --> 00:41:02.200
wouldn't work without it, there's a price.

00:41:02.200 --> 00:41:04.880
Now, perhaps it would have been better.

00:41:04.880 --> 00:41:09.900
The CIA might have been able to go back and
gotten the code machine, if such there exists,

00:41:09.900 --> 00:41:13.490
if we haven't printed the Glomar story.

00:41:13.490 --> 00:41:15.910
But that was the price for a free society.

00:41:15.910 --> 00:41:18.480
Edward Epstein: But what did we gain for that
price?

00:41:18.480 --> 00:41:19.480
Other than one day...

00:41:19.480 --> 00:41:21.240
Jack Nelson: I don't know that we've lost anything.

00:41:21.240 --> 00:41:24.462
Edward Epstein: I don't know that we've lost
anything either but...

00:41:24.462 --> 00:41:26.520
Jack Nelson: You see, we don't even know that
we got anything.

00:41:26.520 --> 00:41:29.200
All we know is what the CIA told us, and that's
all.

00:41:29.200 --> 00:41:30.570
And we do know they don't always tell us the
truth.

00:41:30.570 --> 00:41:31.570
Edward Epstein: But that's my problem.

00:41:31.570 --> 00:41:37.500
I have no way of knowing what a code machine
is, no less how it would be valuable to figuring

00:41:37.500 --> 00:41:39.880
out some Soviet codes or not figuring them
out.

00:41:39.880 --> 00:41:42.170
I agree with you, I'm absolutely ignorant
in this field.

00:41:42.170 --> 00:41:46.060
But then how can we the arbiters of whether
something is legitimate national security

00:41:46.060 --> 00:41:47.060
or not?

00:41:47.060 --> 00:41:49.340
That's the dilemma, as I see it that, you
know, someone has to...

00:41:49.340 --> 00:41:55.650
Floyd Abrams: The solution to that is maybe
the press ought to, given a credible government,

00:41:55.650 --> 00:41:56.650
sometimes believe it.

00:41:56.650 --> 00:41:57.650
Edward Epstein: Yeah.

00:41:57.650 --> 00:42:00.580
Floyd Abrams: And I mean, I have no objection
to Mr. Colby going around Washington trying

00:42:00.580 --> 00:42:03.600
to persuade people, 
I think the press ought to make the decision about

00:42:03.600 --> 00:42:04.600
whether to believe it.

00:42:04.600 --> 00:42:07.550
Jack Nelson: Obviously, a lot of editors did
believe him, because they didn't run the story

00:42:07.550 --> 00:42:10.790
until finally Jack Anderson broke it on radio
one night.

00:42:10.790 --> 00:42:15.040
So, obviously, a lot of editors and a lot
of television news executives did believe

00:42:15.040 --> 00:42:16.040
Mr. Colby.

00:42:16.040 --> 00:42:21.360
Antonin Scalia: Well, I think this analysis of
the Glomar thing as to the possible harm it

00:42:21.360 --> 00:42:27.020
could have done is really not as profound
as it ought to be.

00:42:27.020 --> 00:42:32.770
What action the Russians can take if they
already know it may be quite different from

00:42:32.770 --> 00:42:37.000
what actions the Russians can take and feel
obliged to take if they already know it and

00:42:37.000 --> 00:42:38.850
the whole world knows that they know it.

00:42:38.850 --> 00:42:43.430
Peter Hackes: It's obvious that freedom of the
press means different things to different

00:42:43.430 --> 00:42:44.580
people.

00:42:44.580 --> 00:42:49.450
Sometimes even the courts have trouble interpreting
this basic constitutional protection, especially

00:42:49.450 --> 00:42:55.160
when there's a conflict among the people's
right to know, protection of individual rights,

00:42:55.160 --> 00:42:56.820
and the safety of the republic.

00:42:56.820 --> 00:43:02.500
Now, to challenge our speakers on the freedom
of the press, let's go to our experts in the

00:43:02.500 --> 00:43:03.500
audience.

00:43:03.500 --> 00:43:09.220
William Ruckelshaus: Without any more introductions
in that, I would like to call on somebody

00:43:09.220 --> 00:43:11.610
from the audience who might have a question.

00:43:11.610 --> 00:43:12.670
Yes, sir?

00:43:12.670 --> 00:43:15.920
Howard: I'm Howard Penniman from Georgetown
University.

00:43:15.920 --> 00:43:21.320
Back in June, the "Los Angeles Times" printed
a story about George MacArthur about the heavy

00:43:21.320 --> 00:43:27.990
bombing of South Vietnam on the last days
of the war.

00:43:27.990 --> 00:43:34.050
And then Mr. Seib wrote an excellent commentary
on it a little later.

00:43:34.050 --> 00:43:40.350
And then last week, on the 25th, the "Los
Angeles Times" wrote a retraction of it.

00:43:40.350 --> 00:43:42.990
Indeed, George MacArthur himself wrote the
retraction.

00:43:42.990 --> 00:43:47.490
And the Post, on the following day, printed
a note on the retraction.

00:43:47.490 --> 00:43:52.060
The Post in printing the note said that it
was clear that the White House and the Pentagon

00:43:52.060 --> 00:43:57.790
did not have any responsibility for any significant
bombing.

00:43:57.790 --> 00:44:03.380
What the Post did not include was included
in the "Los Angeles Times", which was that

00:44:03.380 --> 00:44:10.970
all the efforts made by the reporter in Washington
could find no evidence of any significant

00:44:10.970 --> 00:44:14.920
bombing, in fact, two have been ordered by
anyone.

00:44:14.920 --> 00:44:21.761
Furthermore, which leaves still some questions
on how far did the "Los Angeles Times", or

00:44:21.761 --> 00:44:28.660
should the "Los Angeles Times" go in protecting
something which you more or less laughingly

00:44:28.660 --> 00:44:34.520
referred to as American reputation or American
face, and that is involved in this?

00:44:34.520 --> 00:44:39.800
Is it enough to simply absolve the President?

00:44:39.800 --> 00:44:46.540
Is it enough to simply say that yes, we have
now said there was not significant bombing,

00:44:46.540 --> 00:44:52.590
which still leaves it implied that there was
some kind of bombing, in fact, which is not

00:44:52.590 --> 00:44:53.590
yet demonstrated?

00:44:53.590 --> 00:44:57.090
All of this from one of these wonderful sources
which we are to protect.

00:44:57.090 --> 00:45:03.090
I would like to hear Mr. Scalia on anything
about whether he believes this is adequate

00:45:03.090 --> 00:45:08.750
for the protection of national security, whether
their judgments appear to be good, or either

00:45:08.750 --> 00:45:13.670
the others on what is a defense of such actions.

00:45:13.670 --> 00:45:21.570
Antonin Scalia: I don't want to answer that I
guess, for the reason that I really sort of

00:45:21.570 --> 00:45:30.670
jump off the boat as far as the proper role
of government is concerned when it comes to

00:45:30.670 --> 00:45:36.030
correcting the news media, being sure that
what they are reporting they're reporting

00:45:36.030 --> 00:45:38.020
accurately and correctly.

00:45:38.020 --> 00:45:39.020
That's none of my business.

00:45:39.020 --> 00:45:41.410
I don't think it's any of the government's
business.

00:45:41.410 --> 00:45:42.500
That's something quite different.

00:45:42.500 --> 00:45:46.600
What we were talking about earlier was just
the simple questions of can the government

00:45:46.600 --> 00:45:52.890
obtains the information it needs that is necessary
for the administration of justice, and can

00:45:52.890 --> 00:45:58.270
the government in some situations at least,
prevent the dissemination of information that

00:45:58.270 --> 00:46:02.410
would be detrimental to vital national interests?

00:46:02.410 --> 00:46:08.570
Those two questions are quite different from
the problem of if you want to call it irresponsibility

00:46:08.570 --> 00:46:10.100
of the press.

00:46:10.100 --> 00:46:16.930
I think we've made a decision on the First
Amendment that they can be as irresponsible

00:46:16.930 --> 00:46:25.410
in what they choose to say as they wish and
that the corrective is the voice of those

00:46:25.410 --> 00:46:30.450
that are responsible and can correct them,
and to some extent, the libel laws, I suppose.

00:46:30.450 --> 00:46:35.401
Jack Nelson: Well, I would agree with Mr. Scalia
that there's not something for the government

00:46:35.401 --> 00:46:39.970
to take any action on, or anyone else other
than the newspaper itself.

00:46:39.970 --> 00:46:44.650
Now, the government did take action, in that
the government not only denied it but pursued

00:46:44.650 --> 00:46:45.650
its denial.

00:46:45.650 --> 00:46:49.900
As a matter of fact, I happened to run into
Ron Nessen, the President's press secretary,

00:46:49.900 --> 00:46:55.280
at a bar on the evening before the retraction
came out.

00:46:55.280 --> 00:46:59.450
It was not just the day before but some time
before, and Nessen told me at that time, he

00:46:59.450 --> 00:47:04.610
said, "You had the story..." and he said,
"We called in and we told the "Los Angeles

00:47:04.610 --> 00:47:08.420
Times" that the White House unequivocally
denied that the bombing had taken place.

00:47:08.420 --> 00:47:13.130
And he said, "Yet, you ran the story, and
down in the story you ran the denial parenthetically."

00:47:13.130 --> 00:47:14.590
He said, "We think that's wrong."

00:47:14.590 --> 00:47:17.310
And I said, "Well, I don't know any more about
it than what you've just told me and what

00:47:17.310 --> 00:47:21.520
I have seen in the paper, and if we don't
have more to substantiate it than that, I

00:47:21.520 --> 00:47:23.730
think you are right; I think it is wrong."

00:47:23.730 --> 00:47:26.250
And he said he was gonna write to the editor,
which I'm sure he did.

00:47:26.250 --> 00:47:32.380
Now, Charles Seib did write an editorial column
on it, as the ombudsman for the "Washington

00:47:32.380 --> 00:47:37.160
Post", in which he said the "LA Times" had
never been able to substantiate its original

00:47:37.160 --> 00:47:38.160
story.

00:47:38.160 --> 00:47:41.360
Bill Thomas, the editor of the paper, took
it very seriously.

00:47:41.360 --> 00:47:42.800
He looked into it.

00:47:42.800 --> 00:47:47.300
George MacArthur, who had wrote the story
and was in our Saigon bureau, came back to

00:47:47.300 --> 00:47:48.300
the United States.

00:47:48.300 --> 00:47:54.750
He spent two to three weeks trying to prove
whether the information was wrong or whether

00:47:54.750 --> 00:47:58.120
it had been right and the government denial
was wrong.

00:47:58.120 --> 00:48:02.330
He could never really get any substantiation
for it and he admitted it and he wrote the

00:48:02.330 --> 00:48:03.330
story.

00:48:03.330 --> 00:48:07.580
But I think the point is, and Bill Thomas
said this in the article that MacArthur wrote,

00:48:07.580 --> 00:48:11.800
Bill Thomas said that the "Los Angeles Times"
was wrong.

00:48:11.800 --> 00:48:15.700
It should not have run the story because it
got an unequivocal denial, that it should

00:48:15.700 --> 00:48:17.900
have gone back to MacArthur.

00:48:17.900 --> 00:48:22.020
And this is not really an excuse but it's
what happened.

00:48:22.020 --> 00:48:26.010
The communication never got back to MacArthur
that there was an absolute unequivocal denial

00:48:26.010 --> 00:48:28.840
and the story ran with the denial.

00:48:28.840 --> 00:48:31.010
Bill Thomas says the paper should have never
run it.

00:48:31.010 --> 00:48:36.070
We ran a page one correction, as Charles Seib
said, probably the longest retraction in the

00:48:36.070 --> 00:48:37.070
history of journalism.

00:48:37.070 --> 00:48:39.500
"The Washington Post" ran it on page one and
the "New York Times" picked it up and ran

00:48:39.500 --> 00:48:40.500
it.

00:48:40.500 --> 00:48:41.850
And I think we should have run it.

00:48:41.850 --> 00:48:45.520
I don't know what else you can do beyond that,
but I think it's a good reflection of what

00:48:45.520 --> 00:48:49.770
newspapers are addressing themselves to now
and a matter of responsibility on these things.

00:48:49.770 --> 00:48:54.580
So we've got an ombudsman now, for example,
or at least a media critic, by the name of

00:48:54.580 --> 00:48:55.580
David Shaw.

00:48:55.580 --> 00:49:00.910
He does the same sort of criticism, in a sense,
that Charles Seib does and he frequently writes

00:49:00.910 --> 00:49:02.160
a story that we run on page one.

00:49:02.160 --> 00:49:08.230
And he has criticized the "Los Angeles Times",
as well as other parts of the news media when

00:49:08.230 --> 00:49:10.350
he has found that they've been in error.

00:49:10.350 --> 00:49:13.110
But I don't know what else we can do on it
other than correctives.

00:49:13.110 --> 00:49:17.940
I mean, you can't...I suppose if a man got
too far out and was involved in the story,

00:49:17.940 --> 00:49:19.630
he might run the risk of his job.

00:49:19.630 --> 00:49:25.410
I'm sure that whoever was on the desk and
who didn't get the message back to MacArthur

00:49:25.410 --> 00:49:28.960
or something, he's probably heard from the
editor about it.

00:49:28.960 --> 00:49:30.600
I don't know what else you can do though.

00:49:30.600 --> 00:49:35.810
Charles Seib: I'd like to comment on that because
I think that that whole episode raises a couple

00:49:35.810 --> 00:49:38.600
of points that go far beyond the story.

00:49:38.600 --> 00:49:42.740
First of all, one of the main reasons I wrote
the column was not because I knew whether

00:49:42.740 --> 00:49:46.280
the story was right or wrong, because I didn't
at that time.

00:49:46.280 --> 00:49:50.640
But I had a feeling that neither the Post
nor the Times was ever going to tell us whether

00:49:50.640 --> 00:49:51.640
it was right or wrong.

00:49:51.640 --> 00:49:56.310
I think the press has been very reluctant
to clean up its own messes and both the Post

00:49:56.310 --> 00:50:03.140
and the Times led with that story on Sunday
morning, and making a charge, sticking a couple

00:50:03.140 --> 00:50:05.440
of parenthetical denials in it.

00:50:05.440 --> 00:50:09.220
And I knew that it was gonna just die there
unless somebody gave them a push.

00:50:09.220 --> 00:50:13.260
Edward Epstein: Well, you say you don't know
what the press could do about publishing something

00:50:13.260 --> 00:50:14.930
that might be a falsehood.

00:50:14.930 --> 00:50:19.350
Now, I think there is a very simple thing
the press could do and it doesn't involve

00:50:19.350 --> 00:50:21.230
the government at all.

00:50:21.230 --> 00:50:24.560
The journalist could simply publish his source.

00:50:24.560 --> 00:50:30.030
In this case, if MacArthur had published who
had told him that the United States had bombed

00:50:30.030 --> 00:50:33.880
Vietnam on that day, that source would be
quickly discredited.

00:50:33.880 --> 00:50:35.880
Let me give you an example.

00:50:35.880 --> 00:50:40.760
There was a charge made that Alex Butterfield
was a CIA agent in the White House.

00:50:40.760 --> 00:50:44.880
The "New York Times", to its credit, if it
didn't name its source, it intimated so strongly

00:50:44.880 --> 00:50:47.890
that its source was this man Fletcher Prouty.

00:50:47.890 --> 00:50:48.890
Was that his name?

00:50:48.890 --> 00:50:49.890
Floyd Abrams: Yeah.

00:50:49.890 --> 00:50:53.280
Edward Epstein: That very quickly people zeroed
in on him and it became clear that he named

00:50:53.280 --> 00:50:55.520
his source then as E. Howard Hunt.

00:50:55.520 --> 00:51:01.950
And very quickly E. Howard Hunt then denied
that he knew anything at all about Butterfield,

00:51:01.950 --> 00:51:04.671
and Butterfield was cleared without a major
calamity.

00:51:04.671 --> 00:51:07.540
Jack Nelson: George MacArthur would have no
sources if he identified a resource where,

00:51:07.540 --> 00:51:08.540
you must understand that.

00:51:08.540 --> 00:51:09.830
Edward Epstein: Well, but is it better that the
public have the truth?

00:51:09.830 --> 00:51:12.190
Who's the client of newspapers?

00:51:12.190 --> 00:51:13.810
Is it the public or is it the source?

00:51:13.810 --> 00:51:17.520
If you're trying to protect the source or
the reporter, sure it's better for the reporter

00:51:17.520 --> 00:51:18.800
if he never names his source.

00:51:18.800 --> 00:51:22.520
But what about the 10 million readers of the
"Los Angeles Times" who read that...

00:51:22.520 --> 00:51:23.520
Jack Nelson: They will know the story was wrong.

00:51:23.520 --> 00:51:24.590
What else can you do?

00:51:24.590 --> 00:51:25.820
Edward Epstein: They might not.

00:51:25.820 --> 00:51:29.020
They might not have read...the same people
who read the paper on one day might not have

00:51:29.020 --> 00:51:30.670
read it on another day.

00:51:30.670 --> 00:51:33.830
Also, the "Los Angeles Times" denial wasn't
unequivocal.

00:51:33.830 --> 00:51:37.960
It said that no significant bombing we were
able to establish, so it becomes much more

00:51:37.960 --> 00:51:38.960
confusing.

00:51:38.960 --> 00:51:45.090
William Ruckelshaus: Let me ask Mr. Nelson, like
what effect, you indicate that this responsibility

00:51:45.090 --> 00:51:52.100
to act responsibly should primarily be left
to the press, if not exclusively left to the

00:51:52.100 --> 00:51:53.100
press.

00:51:53.100 --> 00:51:57.790
What effect do you think the competition for
news has on the exercise of this responsibility?

00:51:57.790 --> 00:52:00.550
Jack Nelson: Well, I think it has a tremendous
effect.

00:52:00.550 --> 00:52:05.220
And not only that, I think the whole diversification
of the media had a tremendous effect.

00:52:05.220 --> 00:52:06.300
I mean, if we go...

00:52:06.300 --> 00:52:07.300
William Ruckelshaus: Positive or negative?

00:52:07.300 --> 00:52:08.300
Jack Nelson: Well, it's positive.

00:52:08.300 --> 00:52:09.470
I don't think there is any question about
that.

00:52:09.470 --> 00:52:13.290
I mean if we go way out on a limb on something,
as we did on the Vietnam bombing, you know,

00:52:13.290 --> 00:52:15.580
we're gonna get caught short on it.

00:52:15.580 --> 00:52:19.620
You've got all of the news magazines, you've
got the networks, you've got the large newspapers,

00:52:19.620 --> 00:52:24.271
you've got all the wire services, AP, UP,
the supplemental news services, and if you

00:52:24.271 --> 00:52:30.580
really make a substantial error on a matter
of some importance, you're gonna be...the

00:52:30.580 --> 00:52:32.320
public is gonna know about it.

00:52:32.320 --> 00:52:37.140
And it's gonna know about it beyond Mr. Seib's
work because other publications will find

00:52:37.140 --> 00:52:38.140
out about it.

00:52:38.140 --> 00:52:39.680
And you can't retain your...

00:52:39.680 --> 00:52:43.200
William Ruckelshaus: How often do other publications
point out the errors made by...

00:52:43.200 --> 00:52:45.860
Jack Nelson: On matters of substance, I think
they do it frequently.

00:52:45.860 --> 00:52:49.980
They may not say that this was an error, but
they print the story as it really happens,

00:52:49.980 --> 00:52:51.350
which you know...

00:52:51.350 --> 00:52:57.950
William Ruckelshaus: To what extent do you think
the competition that occurs forces you to

00:52:57.950 --> 00:53:01.810
come out with stories before you are able
to completely verify their accuracy?

00:53:01.810 --> 00:53:04.490
Jack Nelson: Well, I think it happens sometimes
and I think it's regrettable.

00:53:04.490 --> 00:53:06.090
I don't think it happens very often.

00:53:06.090 --> 00:53:09.130
I mean, I can remember a few very bad examples.

00:53:09.130 --> 00:53:13.410
I can remember, for example, the Eagleton
matter involving Jack Anderson, which I thought

00:53:13.410 --> 00:53:15.580
was bad and which he later admitted was bad.

00:53:15.580 --> 00:53:20.020
But how many instances can you think of on
a really major matter where a newspaper or

00:53:20.020 --> 00:53:24.110
radio or television came out with something
of real importance that was wrong because

00:53:24.110 --> 00:53:25.620
it was trying to be first?

00:53:25.620 --> 00:53:27.130
When they do it, it's inexcusable.

00:53:27.130 --> 00:53:28.130
William Ruckelshaus: Sure.

00:53:28.130 --> 00:53:33.140
Let me give the Agnew case involving the vice
president of the United States.

00:53:33.140 --> 00:53:38.790
The competition among the press was fierce
in that case.

00:53:38.790 --> 00:53:44.580
And they were all over the Justice Department
halls and the U.S. attorney's office in Baltimore,

00:53:44.580 --> 00:53:49.580
plus the vice president's office himself,
trying to get information on an ongoing investigation

00:53:49.580 --> 00:53:53.070
for which there was no cover-up.

00:53:53.070 --> 00:53:54.080
Many of the stories...

00:53:54.080 --> 00:53:57.590
Jack Nelson: All of that happening, however,
in the aftermath of Watergate when people

00:53:57.590 --> 00:53:58.730
would have been expecting a cover-up.

00:53:58.730 --> 00:54:00.300
William Ruckelshaus: Yeah, I am not saying that
it wasn't understandable, but that doesn't

00:54:00.300 --> 00:54:01.300
make it justifiable.

00:54:01.300 --> 00:54:02.300
Jack Nelson: It doesn't.

00:54:02.300 --> 00:54:03.300
I agree with you.

00:54:03.300 --> 00:54:05.620
William Ruckelshaus: Many of the stories that
were published were full of inaccuracies.

00:54:05.620 --> 00:54:16.920
Charles Seib: Don't you think the Butterfield-Prouty
incident might not have happened if the "NBC"

00:54:16.920 --> 00:54:18.770
and "CBS" hadn't been so competitive?

00:54:18.770 --> 00:54:21.000
Jack Nelson: Yeah, I think that's possible.

00:54:21.000 --> 00:54:25.330
And I think, you know, there are cases, but
I don't think there are that many major cases

00:54:25.330 --> 00:54:26.600
where you find this.

00:54:26.600 --> 00:54:28.490
And when it does happen, it's absolutely inexcusable.

00:54:28.490 --> 00:54:34.510
I don't think there's any way to say that
newspapers or television are right ever when

00:54:34.510 --> 00:54:36.270
they try to be first and they're wrong.

00:54:36.270 --> 00:54:38.630
And I don't think that's the trend, either.

00:54:38.630 --> 00:54:39.990
I think the trend is in the opposite direction.

00:54:39.990 --> 00:54:44.450
I know it's the trend, for example, on the
"Los Angeles Times", and it has been for a

00:54:44.450 --> 00:54:45.450
long time.

00:54:45.450 --> 00:54:47.290
We've been told for example in the Washington
bureau, look.

00:54:47.290 --> 00:54:51.560
And I've had particular cases where I would
be on the story and say, ""The New York Times"

00:54:51.560 --> 00:54:56.110
is here, the "Washington Post" is here," and
this was in the past few years.

00:54:56.110 --> 00:54:59.220
And the editor on the end of it was just,
"Hold on, don't get the story.

00:54:59.220 --> 00:55:00.820
Now get it, be sure you get it all.

00:55:00.820 --> 00:55:02.300
Be sure it's absolutely right.

00:55:02.300 --> 00:55:03.580
We don't care if they're first."

00:55:03.580 --> 00:55:06.360
Now, everybody wants to be first, you know,
there's no question about that.

00:55:06.360 --> 00:55:08.430
But I don't think that's the trend in journalism
in this country.

00:55:08.430 --> 00:55:10.420
I think the trend is to be right, not first.

00:55:10.420 --> 00:55:13.820
William Ruckelshaus: "Don't get it right, get
it written," is no longer screamed in the

00:55:13.820 --> 00:55:14.820
city.

00:55:14.820 --> 00:55:17.510
Jack Nelson: I think that's right.

00:55:17.510 --> 00:55:21.710
Antonin Scalia: Mr. Ruckelshaus is not suggesting,
I take it, that we'd be better off if we had

00:55:21.710 --> 00:55:22.710
only one newspaper.

00:55:22.710 --> 00:55:27.820
William Ruckelshaus: No, I'm not, but I think
that it often is missed on the part of the

00:55:27.820 --> 00:55:32.880
public that the competition for news, particularly
here in Washington, is very strong.

00:55:32.880 --> 00:55:40.230
And that this competition itself has a way
of creating its own momentum for a story to

00:55:40.230 --> 00:55:43.730
be published before there is adequate opportunity
to verify it.

00:55:43.730 --> 00:55:46.160
And having been involved in the middle of
this Agnew investigation, I thought that was

00:55:46.160 --> 00:55:52.400
a prime example of where "The New York Times"
would editorialize about the Justice Department

00:55:52.400 --> 00:55:57.600
leaking information which we were unable to
find in the newspaper, as though this was

00:55:57.600 --> 00:55:58.600
awful.

00:55:58.600 --> 00:56:00.450
Why don't they protect this poor man's rights?

00:56:00.450 --> 00:56:05.270
And then they unleash a horde of reporters
in that same department trying to get...there

00:56:05.270 --> 00:56:07.120
were certain Olympian...

00:56:07.120 --> 00:56:08.120
Floyd Abrams: Wasn't that the "Washington Post"?

00:56:08.120 --> 00:56:09.520
William Ruckelshaus: No, that was the "New York
Times".

00:56:09.520 --> 00:56:13.160
Jack Nelson: Don't you agree, though, that
there have been unusual times in Washington

00:56:13.160 --> 00:56:17.540
in the past few years, with Watergate and
the fact that the resistance of government

00:56:17.540 --> 00:56:22.300
to give out information of legitimate public
interest, information that should have been

00:56:22.300 --> 00:56:23.300
given out?

00:56:23.300 --> 00:56:27.020
It took 18 months to find out who was on the
White House payroll during the Nixon administration.

00:56:27.020 --> 00:56:28.220
I mean, these matters you could go down to...

00:56:28.220 --> 00:56:29.390
William Ruckelshaus: I'll bet you still haven't
found out.

00:56:29.390 --> 00:56:30.390
Jack Nelson: That's right.

00:56:30.390 --> 00:56:32.100
I think our time is up.

00:56:32.100 --> 00:56:35.290
William Ruckelshaus: I think that we are running
out of time.

00:56:35.290 --> 00:56:41.320
And again, in closing I would like to indicate
my appreciation and that of our panel for

00:56:41.320 --> 00:56:48.150
the audience's participation, and recognize
that while we've all joined here together

00:56:48.150 --> 00:56:54.280
and by no means where we leave in complete
agreement, one of principles that has been

00:56:54.280 --> 00:57:00.540
stood up for here with such great articulation
by the defenders of the press, I believe is

00:57:00.540 --> 00:57:06.390
precisely the reason we're able to meet like
this as free men and women and discuss as

00:57:06.390 --> 00:57:13.080
freely as possible and with little chance
of any interference, issues as important as

00:57:13.080 --> 00:57:17.410
freedom of the press and the future of this
society.

00:57:17.410 --> 00:57:23.030
So, again, on behalf of the panel let me thank
all of you for your participation and thank

00:57:23.030 --> 00:57:31.330
the members of the panel for being here.

00:57:31.330 --> 00:57:42.650
Peter Hackes: This roundtable discussion has
brought you the ideas of five experts who

00:57:42.650 --> 00:57:47.030
have differing views on the many interpretations
of freedom of the press.

00:57:47.030 --> 00:57:52.500
It is the aim of the American Enterprise Institute
to illuminate issues of the day by presenting

00:57:52.500 --> 00:57:58.640
many such views, in the hope that by so doing
those in positions of decision-making will

00:57:58.640 --> 00:58:03.660
benefit from such a free exchange of informed
and enlightened opinion.

00:58:03.660 --> 00:58:06.400
I'm Peter Hackes in Washington.

00:58:06.400 --> 00:58:18.830
Announcer: "Public Policy Forums" is created and
supplied to this station as a public service

00:58:18.830 --> 00:58:31.220
by the American Enterprise Institute, Washington,
DC.

